          "type": "concept",
          "title": "Insider Knowledge: Chokepoints Run the Modern World",
          "body": "Modern geopolitics is still largely a fight over a few pieces of geography: narrow straits, canals, and resource corridors. Whoever controls the Suez Canal, the Strait of Malacca, or the pipelines crossing a steppe can throttle global trade without firing a shot. The map looks settled, but tectonics still lift mountains, seas still vanish, and rivers still shift — geography is not a backdrop to history; it keeps writing it.",
